ph:Fluad a idmp:MedicinalProduct ;
    ph:medicalCountermeasureType "vaccine"@en ;
    ph:sourcePage 37 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 37] In addition to centrally EU-level authorized vaccines, seasonal influenza vaccines with national marketing authorisation in the EU/EEA are manufactured by Abbott (Influvac, Influvac Junior, Influvac Tetra, Batrevac and Batrevac Tetra), GSK (Fluarix and Influsplit in both trivalent and quadrivalent forms), Sanofi Pasteur (Vaxigrip, Vaxigrip Tetra, Vaxigrip Infants, Mutagrip and Efluelda) and Seqirus (Fluad, Fluad Tetra and Afluria Tetra). These vaccines are authorised for use across various age groups and are formulated to match the seasonal influenza strains that are recommended annually. Some quadrivalent formulations may be phased out in the future following EMA guidance on the declining relevance of the B/Yamagata lineage."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fluad"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasTherapeuticIndication ph:Fluad_Seasonal_Influenza_PREVENTION_Indication ;
    idmp:isProducedBy ph:Org_Seqirus .

ph:Fluad_Tetra a idmp:MedicinalProduct ;
    ph:medicalCountermeasureType "vaccine"@en ;
    ph:sourcePage 37 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 37] In addition to centrally EU-level authorized vaccines, seasonal influenza vaccines with national marketing authorisation in the EU/EEA are manufactured by Abbott (Influvac, Influvac Junior, Influvac Tetra, Batrevac and Batrevac Tetra), GSK (Fluarix and Influsplit in both trivalent and quadrivalent forms), Sanofi Pasteur (Vaxigrip, Vaxigrip Tetra, Vaxigrip Infants, Mutagrip and Efluelda) and Seqirus (Fluad, Fluad Tetra and Afluria Tetra). These vaccines are authorised for use across various age groups and are formulated to match the seasonal influenza strains that are recommended annually. Some quadrivalent formulations may be phased out in the future following EMA guidance on the declining relevance of the B/Yamagata lineage."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fluad Tetra"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasTherapeuticIndication ph:Fluad_Tetra_Seasonal_Influenza_PREVENTION_Indication ;
    idmp:isProducedBy ph:Org_Seqirus .

ph:Fluarix a idmp:MedicinalProduct ;
    ph:medicalCountermeasureType "vaccine"@en ;
    ph:sourcePage 37 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 37] In addition to centrally EU-level authorized vaccines, seasonal influenza vaccines with national marketing authorisation in the EU/EEA are manufactured by Abbott (Influvac, Influvac Junior, Influvac Tetra, Batrevac and Batrevac Tetra), GSK (Fluarix and Influsplit in both trivalent and quadrivalent forms), Sanofi Pasteur (Vaxigrip, Vaxigrip Tetra, Vaxigrip Infants, Mutagrip and Efluelda) and Seqirus (Fluad, Fluad Tetra and Afluria Tetra). These vaccines are authorised for use across various age groups and are formulated to match the seasonal influenza strains that are recommended annually. Some quadrivalent formulations may be phased out in the future following EMA guidance on the declining relevance of the B/Yamagata lineage."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fluarix"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasTherapeuticIndication ph:Fluarix_Seasonal_Influenza_PREVENTION_Indication ;
    idmp:isProducedBy ph:Org_GSK .

ph:Flucelvax_Tetra a idmp:MedicinalProduct ;
    ph:medicalCountermeasureType "vaccine"@en ;
    ph:sourcePage 37 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 37] Three main types of influenza vaccines have been authorised in the EU: (1) inactivated influenza vaccines (IIVs), which are whole-virus, split or subunit products that trigger antibody production against viral surface antigens (examples include Fluad Tetra and Flucelvax Tetra (Seqirus)); (2) recombinant protein-based vaccines that are produced via DNA-engineered systems to express viral surface proteins (examples include Supemtek Tetra (Sanofi)); and (3) live-attenuated influenza vaccines (LAIVs), which contain genetically weakened but replication-competent influenza viruses that are cold-adapted and temperature-sensitive, allowing limited replication in the nasopharyngeal mucosa but not in the lower respiratory tract (examples include Fluenz Tetra (AstraZeneca))."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Flucelvax Tetra"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasTherapeuticIndication ph:Flucelvax_Tetra_Seasonal_Influenza_PREVENTION_Indication ;
    idmp:isProducedBy ph:Org_Seqirus .

ph:Fluenz_Tetra a idmp:MedicinalProduct ;
    ph:medicalCountermeasureType "vaccine"@en ;
    ph:sourcePage 37 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 37] Three main types of influenza vaccines have been authorised in the EU: (1) inactivated influenza vaccines (IIVs), which are whole-virus, split or subunit products that trigger antibody production against viral surface antigens (examples include Fluad Tetra and Flucelvax Tetra (Seqirus)); (2) recombinant protein-based vaccines that are produced via DNA-engineered systems to express viral surface proteins (examples include Supemtek Tetra (Sanofi)); and (3) live-attenuated influenza vaccines (LAIVs), which contain genetically weakened but replication-competent influenza viruses that are cold-adapted and temperature-sensitive, allowing limited replication in the nasopharyngeal mucosa but not in the lower respiratory tract (examples include Fluenz Tetra (AstraZeneca))."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fluenz Tetra"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasTherapeuticIndication ph:Fluenz_Tetra_Seasonal_Influenza_PREVENTION_Indication ;
    idmp:isProducedBy ph:Org_AstraZeneca .

ph:Flumadine a idmp:MedicinalProduct ;
    ph:hasActiveIngredient ph:API_rimantadine ;
    ph:medicalCountermeasureType "antiviral medication"@en,
        "medication"@en ;
    ph:sourcePage 39,
        40 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 39] Adamantanes (amantadine and rimantadine) inhibit the influenza A M2 ion channel. They were previously used for treatment and prophylaxis of influenza A infections but have now been effectively abandoned because of the near-universal resistance among the currently circulating human influenza A(H1N1)pdm09 and A(H3N2) viruses (34). This resistance is primarily mediated by the S31N substitution in the M2 protein."@en,
        "[p. 40] Flumadine (national authorisation only)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Not active against influenza B; near-universal resistance in circulating A(H1N1)pdm09 and A(H3N2); generally not recommended."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Flumadine"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasTherapeuticIndication ph:Flumadine_Avian_Influenza_TREATMENT_Indication,
        ph:Flumadine_Seasonal_Influenza_TREATMENT_Indication .

