ph:MBP134 a idmp:MedicinalProduct ;
    ph:medicalCountermeasureType "medication"@en,
        "monoclonal antibody"@en ;
    ph:sourcePage 52 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 52] There are no licensed therapeutics for Sudan ebolavirus, but several candidates are under development (most notably the monoclonal antibody MBP134 a cocktail of two broadly neutralising human monoclonal antibodies developed by the Sabin Vaccine Institute and partners, which has demonstrated protective efficacy in preclinical studies against both Ebola and Sudan viruses -specific mAb and investigational antivirals like remdesivir). Ongoing efforts are being Blueprint."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "MBP134"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasTherapeuticIndication ph:MBP134_Sudan_Virus_Disease_TREATMENT_Indication .

ph:MERSCoV a ph:InfectiousDiseaseOrPathogen,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    ph:hasRouteOfTransmission ph:DirectContact,
        ph:Foodborne,
        ph:Nosocomial,
        ph:Respiratory,
        ph:Zoonotic ;
    ph:hasSusceptibleHost ph:Animals,
        ph:Humans ;
    owl:sameAs eios:MERSCoV ;
    skos:prefLabel "MERSCoV"@en .

ph:MPB091 a idmp:MedicinalProduct ;
    ph:medicalCountermeasureType "medication"@en,
        "monoclonal antibody"@en ;
    ph:sourcePage 52 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 52] Researchers are investigating the use of monoclonal antibodies for the treatment of MVD. These include MR191-N (a Marburg-specific antibody) as well as Ebola-targeted antibodies such as Inmazeb and Ebanga, which are being explored for their potential against Marburg in preclinical studies. Another promising investigational monoclonal antibody (MPB091) is being studied for its ability to inhibit viral replication."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "MPB091"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasTherapeuticIndication ph:MPB091_Marburg_Virus_Disease_TREATMENT_Indication .

ph:MR191_N a idmp:MedicinalProduct ;
    ph:medicalCountermeasureType "medication"@en,
