ph:m102_4 a idmp:MedicinalProduct ;
    ph:medicalCountermeasureType "medication"@en,
        "monoclonal antibody"@en ;
    ph:sourcePage 59 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 59] The monoclonal antibody m102.4, which has completed Phase 1 clinical trials, has been used for compassionate use during outbreaks. However, clinical evidence supporting the use of remdesivir or m102.4 in humans is very limited and remains investigational."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "m102.4"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasTherapeuticIndication ph:m102_4_Hendra_Virus_Disease_TREATMENT_Indication,
        ph:m102_4_Nipah_Virus_Disease_TREATMENT_Indication .

ph:mRNA_1215 a idmp:MedicinalProduct ;
    ph:medicalCountermeasureType "vaccine"@en ;
    ph:sourcePage 58 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 58] There is currently no licensed vaccine for the Nipah virus or the Hendra virus in humans. Several vaccine candidates are in early clinical development. These include (i) the HeV-sG-V, a recombinant subunit vaccine that targets both Hendra and Nipah viruses; (ii) -1215, an mRNA vaccine which encodes the Nipah virus attachment glycoprotein; and (iii) University of -vector vaccine based on the ChAdOx1 platform. In addition, (iv) PHV02, a recombinant VSV-based vaccine expressing glycoproteins from Ebola and Nipah viruses, has been described in preclinical development, although, as of October 2025, no confirmed public record indicates progression to a human Phase 1 clinical trial."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "mRNA-1215"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasTherapeuticIndication ph:mRNA_1215_Nipah_Virus_Disease_PREVENTION_Indication ;
    idmp:isProducedBy ph:Org_Moderna .

ph:medicalCountermeasureType a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdf:langString ;
    skos:prefLabel "medical countermeasure type"@en .

ph:nOPV a idmp:MedicinalProduct ;
    ph:medicalCountermeasureType "vaccine"@en ;
    ph:sourcePage 77 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 77] A newer version of OPV (novel oral poliovirus vaccine nOPV) has been developed to reduce the risk of vaccine- This is primarily used for type 2 poliovirus, where the risk of vaccine-derived outbreaks has been more common."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Developed to reduce the risk of vaccine-derived outbreaks and used primarily for type 2 poliovirus."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Novel oral poliovirus vaccine"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasTherapeuticIndication ph:nOPV_Poliomyelitis_PREVENTION_Indication .

ph:rVSV_DG_LASV_GPC a idmp:MedicinalProduct ;
    ph:medicalCountermeasureType "vaccine"@en ;
    ph:sourcePage 64,
        65 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 64] -LASV-GPC became the first Lassa fever vaccine candidate to receive PRIME designation from EMA. This designation is intended to expedite the development of medicines that address unmet medical needs, particularly those that show early clinical"@en,
        "[p. 65] promise. The WHO and CEPI have prioritised vaccine development against Lassa infection and several candidates in preclinical and clinical development exist. Candid#1, a live-attenuated Junin virus vaccine is only licensed in Argentina."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "rVSVΔG-LASV-GPC"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasTherapeuticIndication ph:rVSV_DG_LASV_GPC_Lassa_Fever_PREVENTION_Indication .

