ph:Droplet a ph:RouteOfTransmission,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "Droplet transmission is the spread of an infectious agent or disease caused by the dissemination of respiratory droplets that are expelled when an infected individual coughs, sneezes, talks, or breathes."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Droplet Transmission"@en .

ph:Dyspnoea a ph:SignOrSymptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:comment "Dyspnoea is used to describe perceptions of difficulty or distress related to breathing and is recognised as symptomatic of disease when it occurs under inappropriate circumstances. Dyspnoea is a presenting complaint of patients with a wide variety of medical diseases by multiple mechanisms. Dyspnoea is considered acute when it lasts from hours up to 3 weeks, subacute from 3 weeks up to 8 weeks, and chronic dyspnoea lasts more than 8 weeks."@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Shortness of Breath"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dyspnoea"@en .

ph:Ebanga_Ebola_Virus_Disease_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 51,
        52 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 51] - Ebanga (Ansuvimab-zykl/mAb114; Ridgeback Biotherapeutics, LP) is a monoclonal antibody that targets the Zaire ebolavirus glycoprotein and is administered as a single intravenous injection. The FDA approved Ebanga in December 2020."@en,
        "[p. 52] Researchers are investigating the use of monoclonal antibodies for the treatment of MVD. These include MR191-N (a Marburg-specific antibody) as well as Ebola-targeted antibodies such as Inmazeb and Ebanga, which are being explored for their potential against Marburg in preclinical studies. Another promising investigational monoclonal antibody (MPB091) is being studied for its ability to inhibit viral replication."@en ;
    dcterms:date "2020-12"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Ebola ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn ph:UnitedStates .

ph:Ebanga_Marburg_Virus_Disease_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 52 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 52] Researchers are investigating the use of monoclonal antibodies for the treatment of MVD. These include MR191-N (a Marburg-specific antibody) as well as Ebola-targeted antibodies such as Inmazeb and Ebanga, which are being explored for their potential against Marburg in preclinical studies. Another promising investigational monoclonal antibody (MPB091) is being studied for its ability to inhibit viral replication."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Being explored preclinically for potential activity against Marburg virus."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:MVD ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn ph:Global .

ph:Ebilfumin_Avian_Influenza_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 39,
        40 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 39] Several antiviral drugs are available and authorised to treat influenza upon disease onset. Zoonotic influenza are generally susceptible to the same drugs. It is recommended that antivirals should be administered as early as possible because, in later phases of the disease, the host response rather than the direct effects of the virus will increasingly determine the course and outcome of the disease. Alternatively, some antivirals are also approved for pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) in exceptional circumstances (such as when there is a mismatch between the circulating and vaccine virus strains or during a pandemic situation) and for post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP)."@en,
        "[p. 40] Ebilflumin, Actavis Group Oseltamivir"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Zoonotic influenza is generally susceptible to seasonal-influenza antivirals; use should follow national and WHO guidance."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Avianflu ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn cmns-ge-euj:EuropeanUnion .

ph:Ebilfumin_Seasonal_Influenza_PEP_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 39,
        40 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 39] Several antiviral drugs are available and authorised to treat influenza upon disease onset. Zoonotic influenza are generally susceptible to the same drugs. It is recommended that antivirals should be administered as early as possible because, in later phases of the disease, the host response rather than the direct effects of the virus will increasingly determine the course and outcome of the disease. Alternatively, some antivirals are also approved for pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) in exceptional circumstances (such as when there is a mismatch between the circulating and vaccine virus strains or during a pandemic situation) and for post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP)."@en,
        "[p. 40] Ebilflumin, Actavis Group Oseltamivir"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Seasonal_Influenza ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn cmns-ge-euj:EuropeanUnion .

