ph:IAVI_SUDV_GP_Sudan_Virus_Disease_PREVENTION_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 51 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 51] There are currently no licensed vaccines for Sudan ebolavirus, but several candidates are under development. In particular, the Sabin Vaccine Institute has initiated Phase 2 clinical trials for its Sudan ebolavirus vaccine in Kenya and Uganda. The International AIDS Vaccine Initiative (IAVI) is (in partnership with Merck and supported by CEPI and BARDA) developing a Sudan virus vaccine candidate (IAVI SUDV-GP), which is based on the VSV platform that is also used in the licensed Zaire ebolavirus vaccine (Ervebo). The US FDA has granted orphan d and post-exposure prophylaxis against Sudan ebolavirus infection."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "VSV-platform candidate for pre- and post-exposure prophylaxis; the report notes US FDA orphan designation."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Sudan_Virus_Disease ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn ph:UnitedStates .

ph:INO_4700_MERS_Disease_PREVENTION_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 32 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 32] HCoV-HKU1) as of October 2025. However, two MERS-CoV vaccine candidates (ChAdOx1-MERS and INO-4700) were in Phase III trials under CEPI-WHO collaboration."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:MERS_Disease ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn ph:Global .

ph:IPV_Poliomyelitis_PREVENTION_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 77 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 77] The primary vaccine against polio that is authorised and recommended for use in the EU/EEA is the inactivated poliovirus vaccine (IPV), which is widely used in most countries. The oral poliovirus vaccine (OPV) is still used globally, particularly for outbreak responses and in areas where polio remains a concern."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Poliomyelitis ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn cmns-ge-euj:EuropeanUnion .

ph:Imvanex_Mpox_Disease_PREVENTION_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 55 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 55] Third-generation vaccines are based on non-replicating attenuated strains of vaccinia virus. They offer a better safety profile and suitability for immunocompromised individuals. Imvanex (Bavarian Nordic) is the only vaccine currently authorised in the EU for prevention of both smallpox and mpox in adults. It is a non-replicating and live-attenuated third-generation vaccine based on the Modified Vaccinia Ankara Bavarian Nordic (MVA-BN) virus. It is to be administered subcutaneously in a standalone 2-dose treatment."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Authorised under exceptional circumstances."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Monkeypox ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn cmns-ge-euj:EuropeanUnion .

ph:Imvanex_Smallpox_Disease_PREVENTION_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 55 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 55] Third-generation vaccines are based on non-replicating attenuated strains of vaccinia virus. They offer a better safety profile and suitability for immunocompromised individuals. Imvanex (Bavarian Nordic) is the only vaccine currently authorised in the EU for prevention of both smallpox and mpox in adults. It is a non-replicating and live-attenuated third-generation vaccine based on the Modified Vaccinia Ankara Bavarian Nordic (MVA-BN) virus. It is to be administered subcutaneously in a standalone 2-dose treatment."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Authorised under exceptional circumstances."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Smallpox ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn cmns-ge-euj:EuropeanUnion .

ph:Incellipan_Avian_Influenza_PREVENTION_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 38 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 38] Four pandemic preparedness vaccines (formerly known as mock up vaccines) are currently authorised in the EU. These can be modified into pandemic influenza vaccines in future pandemics: Foclivia (Seqirus), Adjupanrix (GSK), pandemic influenza vaccine H5N1 (AstraZeneca) and Incellipan (Seqirus) (33). As of October 2025, all were still listed as pandemic prototype or strain-change -ready vaccines under EMA supervision, with this regulatory framework allowing antigen updates without a new full authorisation ( strain-change procedure)."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Authorised in advance for pandemic preparedness."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Avianflu ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn cmns-ge-euj:EuropeanUnion .

ph:Influsplit_Seasonal_Influenza_PREVENTION_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 37 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 37] In addition to centrally EU-level authorized vaccines, seasonal influenza vaccines with national marketing authorisation in the EU/EEA are manufactured by Abbott (Influvac, Influvac Junior, Influvac Tetra, Batrevac and Batrevac Tetra), GSK (Fluarix and Influsplit in both trivalent and quadrivalent forms), Sanofi Pasteur (Vaxigrip, Vaxigrip Tetra, Vaxigrip Infants, Mutagrip and Efluelda) and Seqirus (Fluad, Fluad Tetra and Afluria Tetra). These vaccines are authorised for use across various age groups and are formulated to match the seasonal influenza strains that are recommended annually. Some quadrivalent formulations may be phased out in the future following EMA guidance on the declining relevance of the B/Yamagata lineage."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Seasonal_Influenza ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn cmns-ge-euj:EuropeanUnion .

ph:Influvac_Junior_Seasonal_Influenza_PREVENTION_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
