ph:Regkirona_COVID_19_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 31 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 31] Regkirona (regdanvimab; Celltrion Healthcare), Ronapreve (casirivimab/imdevimab; Regeneron and Roche), and Xevudy (sotrovimab; GSK) are still authorised. However, they show limited neutralising activity against currently circulating variants and are no longer recommended by ECDC clinical-management guidance."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Limited neutralising activity against currently circulating variants; no longer recommended by ECDC clinical-management guidance."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:COVID_19 ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn cmns-ge-euj:EuropeanUnion .

ph:Relenza_Avian_Influenza_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 39,
        40 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 39] Several antiviral drugs are available and authorised to treat influenza upon disease onset. Zoonotic influenza are generally susceptible to the same drugs. It is recommended that antivirals should be administered as early as possible because, in later phases of the disease, the host response rather than the direct effects of the virus will increasingly determine the course and outcome of the disease. Alternatively, some antivirals are also approved for pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) in exceptional circumstances (such as when there is a mismatch between the circulating and vaccine virus strains or during a pandemic situation) and for post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP)."@en,
        "[p. 40] Relenza, GSK Zanamivir"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Zoonotic influenza is generally susceptible to seasonal-influenza antivirals; use should follow national and WHO guidance."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Avianflu ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn cmns-ge-euj:EuropeanUnion .

ph:Relenza_Seasonal_Influenza_PEP_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 40 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 40] Relenza, GSK Zanamivir"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Seasonal_Influenza ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn cmns-ge-euj:EuropeanUnion .

ph:Relenza_Seasonal_Influenza_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 39,
        40 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 39] Several antiviral drugs are available and authorised to treat influenza upon disease onset. Zoonotic influenza are generally susceptible to the same drugs. It is recommended that antivirals should be administered as early as possible because, in later phases of the disease, the host response rather than the direct effects of the virus will increasingly determine the course and outcome of the disease. Alternatively, some antivirals are also approved for pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) in exceptional circumstances (such as when there is a mismatch between the circulating and vaccine virus strains or during a pandemic situation) and for post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP)."@en,
        "[p. 40] Relenza, GSK Zanamivir"@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Seasonal_Influenza ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn cmns-ge-euj:EuropeanUnion .

ph:Ribavirin_Crimean_Congo_Haemorrhagic_Fever_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 74 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 74] focusing on managing symptoms, maintaining fluid balance and treating secondary infections. Efforts to develop specific antiviral treatments or improve the effectiveness of existing options are ongoing. Off-label use of ribavirin, an antiviral drug, has been used with some success in reducing mortality in CCHF cases when administered early, but its efficacy remains inconclusive. Clinical use continues to be off-label and case-dependent."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Used case-by-case, particularly when administered early; efficacy remains inconclusive."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Crimean_Congo_Haemorrhagic_Fever ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn ph:Global .

ph:Ribavirin_Haemorrhagic_Fever_With_Renal_Syndrome_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 71 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 71] Advances have been made in the study of Hantaviridae (including the development of specific therapeutics), but there are no approved antivirals to treat hantavirus infection. Ribavirin has been used off-label in some cases, with mixed clinical outcomes and unclear benefit."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Used off-label in some hantavirus cases with mixed outcomes and unclear benefit."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Haemorrhagic_Fever_With_Renal_Syndrome ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn ph:Global .

ph:Ribavirin_Hendra_Virus_Disease_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 59 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 59] Off-label use of ribavirin has been attempted for Hendra and Nipah viruses in laboratory settings, but its efficacy in humans remains to be demonstrated."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Attempted off-label; efficacy in humans remains unproven."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Hendra_Virus_Disease ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn ph:Global .

ph:Ribavirin_Lassa_Fever_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 24 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 24] In the EU, there is no specific MCM authorised against the priority pathogens of this family. The one exception is the use of ribavirin, which may offer benefit for Lassa fever under compassionate-use or off-label protocols."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "May offer benefit for Lassa fever under compassionate-use or off-label protocols; no EU-authorised arenavirus therapeutic is available."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:LassaFever ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn cmns-ge-euj:EuropeanUnion .

ph:Ribavirin_Nipah_Virus_Disease_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 59 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 59] Off-label use of ribavirin has been attempted for Hendra and Nipah viruses in laboratory settings, but its efficacy in humans remains to be demonstrated."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Attempted off-label in laboratory/clinical contexts; efficacy in humans remains unproven."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Nipah_Virus_Disease ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn ph:Global .

ph:Ribavirin_Rift_Valley_Fever_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 68 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 68] No antiviral treatments are currently approved for the treatment of RVF. Off-label use of RNA-dependent RNA polymerase inhibitor antivirals like ribavirin and favipiravir are under investigation and may, depending on the results, demonstrate potential for future clinical trials investigations."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "RNA-dependent RNA polymerase inhibitor under investigation; no antiviral is approved for RVF."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:RiftValleyFever ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn ph:Global .

ph:Rift_Valley_Fever_Virus skos:prefLabel "Rift Valley fever virus"@en .

ph:RoActemra_COVID_19_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 31 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 31] Tocilizumab (RoActemra; Roche Pharma AG) is an IL-6 receptor blocker indicated for severe COVID-19 with evidence of systemic inflammation."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Indicated for severe COVID-19 with evidence of systemic inflammation."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:COVID_19 ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn cmns-ge-euj:EuropeanUnion .

ph:RoActemra_Hantavirus_Cardiopulmonary_Syndrome_TREATMENT_Indication a idmp:TherapeuticIndication ;
    ph:sourcePage 72 ;
    ph:sourceText "[p. 72] anti-hantavirus mAb; and (2) small-molecule antivirals like favipiravir, which has shown efficacy in animal models and is considered for repurposing despite being used off-label in some viral infections. In addition, immunomodulators like tocilizumab and vandetanib, which were originally developed for other indications, are being explored for severe disease management, particularly to address vascular leakage and cytokine dysregulation in HCPS."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Tocilizumab is being explored for severe hantavirus disease management."@en ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ph:HERA_Virus_Prioritisation_Report ;
    idmp:hasIndicationAsDiseaseSymptomProcedure ph:Hantavirus_Cardiopulmonary_Syndrome ;
    idmp:isApplicableIn ph:Global .

